/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   Property hero-morph transition  (View Transitions API + stagger CSS)
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

   How it works:
   1. Both the Tenant Directory hero and Tenant Profile hero carry the class
      .pt-hero, giving them view-transition-name: property-hero.
   2. document.startViewTransition() captures old + new DOM snapshots.
   3. The browser automatically interpolates position, size, and border-radius
      between the two hero cards.  We only animate the content cross-fade.
   4. The root (everything outside the hero) fades + scales out; the new
      page's secondary elements self-animate with .pt-bounce / .pt-slide-top.
   ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */

/* Tag the morphing element on both pages */
.pt-hero {
  view-transition-name: property-hero;
}

/* ── Hero: content cross-fade inside the morphing card ──────────────── */
::view-transition-old(property-hero) {
  animation: 340ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.2, 1) both pt-fade-out;
}
::view-transition-new(property-hero) {
  animation: 500ms cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1) both pt-fade-in;
}
@keyframes pt-fade-out { to   { opacity: 0; } }
@keyframes pt-fade-in  { from { opacity: 0; } }

/* ── Root (non-hero): old page exits, new page appears bare ─────────── */
::view-transition-old(root) {
  animation: 210ms cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 1, 1) both pt-root-out;
}
::view-transition-new(root) {
  /* New page appears immediately; individual elements animate themselves */
  animation: 1ms linear both;
}
@keyframes pt-root-out {
  to { opacity: 0; transform: scale(0.97) translateY(-6px); }
}

/* ── Bounce pop in — used for profile secondary elements ─────────────── */
@keyframes bouncePopIn {
  0%   { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(30px) scale(0.86); }
  55%  { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(-7px) scale(1.045); }
  74%  { transform: translateY(3px) scale(0.983); }
  88%  { transform: translateY(-1.5px) scale(1.007); }
  100% { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0) scale(1); }
}

/* ── Slide pop from above — used for the top bar ─────────────────────── */
@keyframes slideInFromTop {
  from { opacity: 0; transform: translateY(-18px) scale(0.96); }
  to   { opacity: 1; transform: translateY(0)     scale(1);    }
}

.pt-bounce {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: bouncePopIn 0.52s cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1) both;
  animation-delay: var(--pt-d, 0ms);
}

.pt-slide-top {
  opacity: 0;
  animation: slideInFromTop 0.30s cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1) both;
  animation-delay: var(--pt-d, 0ms);
}

/* ── Respect prefers-reduced-motion ─────────────────────────────────── */
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  ::view-transition-old(property-hero),
  ::view-transition-new(property-hero),
  ::view-transition-old(root),
  ::view-transition-new(root) {
    animation-duration: 0.01ms !important;
  }
  .pt-bounce,
  .pt-slide-top {
    animation: none !important;
    opacity: 1 !important;
  }
}

/* ── Native Apple Pay button ─────────────────────────────────────────── */
.apple-pay-btn {
  -webkit-appearance: -apple-pay-button;
  -apple-pay-button-type: pay;
  -apple-pay-button-style: black;
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  height: 52px;
  min-height: 44px;
  border: none;
  border-radius: 18px;
  cursor: pointer;
  margin-bottom: 10px;
}

content.text:
I found the input bug already. In `index.css` lines 392–406, there's a **global mobile override**:

```css
@media (max-width: 640px) {
  input {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08) !important;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.20) !important;  /* white border */
    color: black !important;
  }
}
```

That was written for the dark glassmorphism terminal, but it's an unscoped `input` selector with `!important`, so on every light-background page on mobile you get a near-white border on a near-white background → invisible box. (Note it also doesn't touch `select`/textarea, so dropdowns rely on the faint `--input` gray.) That's request #2, basically diagnosed.

Now let me understand the "retail has a web area" layout situation before I propose anything.

